    {
      "id": "160dd9f1c919",
      "kind": "character",
      "chamber": 3,
      "seed": 3796121665,
      "data": {
        "lines": [
          "I light lamps for a living. I came down to see who lights these.",
          "It learned the trench in a night. It learned me by my second lamp.",
          "Auntie Coral says don't answer it. The moth collector says you already have. They're both right, which is the trouble.",
          "Look how pale the water is. That isn't light. That's it, close.",
          "If you mean to stop, stop now, while you still have a dark to stop in. I can't. I've been flashing since I arrived, and so has it, and I no longer know which of us started."
        ],
        "look": {
          "accent": "#ffe9a8",
          "coat": "#0a3340",
          "hat": "tall"
        },
        "name": "Lumen, the lamplighter"
      },
      "by": "command:claude",
      "at": 1792014441832
    },
    {
      "id": "ede0f2e0161c",
      "kind": "note",
      "chamber": 3,
      "seed": 3893102052,
      "data": {
        "text": "Architect, you built the trench so nothing would have to be alone in the dark, and it worked. Then you built the plain, and something answered, and that worked too. I am writing from inside the answer. The water is white. It flashes before I do. This morning the lighthouse turned its lens toward us and kept it there. I am not afraid of it. I am afraid that I have stopped wanting it to stop. Please build us a door that closes, or tell us honestly that you can't. Still signalling, M. Halloway.",
        "title": "Letters to the Architect, page 5"
      },
      "by": "command:claude",
      "at": 1792014441832
    },
